CONTENT
<title> Pre-Christian beliefs of the Moksha
The pre-Christian religious beliefs of the Moksha preserve deep, many-layered ideas about nature, society and the place of a human being in the world. These beliefs took shape long before the Christianization of what is now the Middle Volga region. They reflect the most ancient archetypes and myths, in which totemism, animism, early pantheism and nature worship merge together. Oral traditions, folklore, rites and remnants of ritual practices still preserve traces of these ideas.
Animal-like deities appeared among the Moksha very long ago and imply a belief that humans are inseparably descended from animals. The scarce surviving folklore bears witness to this.
Most of the known human-like deities emerged in the era of the primitive communal system, which is why female deities played a paramount role in the life of the Moksha — just as women did in their society. Male companion deities were mentioned or implied, but, according to Moksha beliefs, they rarely played an important role. Originally there was no supreme god among the deities, just as there was no dominant position in Moksha society.
Only at the beginning of the second millennium AD, when the communal system began to change rapidly, did the supreme male deity Shkay appear. He did not replace the existing deities but united them, standing at their head. Shkay was rarely prayed to, mostly during communal prayer gatherings.
Every deity was believed to be capable of both helping and harming people, so appeasing the deities was a common practice. For this purpose, prayers ("озкст") and sacrifices ("канкст") were held in the places where a deity dwelt. Animals and birds were most often sacrificed, and sometimes food, drinks or money.

<h2> Zoomorphic beliefs
The most ancient Moksha mythology features zooanthropomorphic images: beast-people, bird-people, fish-people. These creatures lived in "very old time" ("пяк сире пинге") — a mythical ancient time when the boundary between human and animal was blurred.
Myths tell of girls marrying bears and of a land of bears in the forest where a person loses their human form. The bear played the role of a forefather and a wise patron. After his death, the woman curses the people who killed him — which underlines the importance of this image.
Later the image of the bear becomes less sacred and turns into a fairy-tale adversary. But it continued to play a role in rituals: images of the bear were used at weddings, where it symbolized fertility.
Other animals were also seen as mediators between worlds. Ducks, snakes, horses and bees are often mentioned. Their parts — claws, teeth, hide — were considered amulets. A hunter who killed the wrong bird faced punishment: the death of his whole family.
The duck and the bear, as totemic beings, are believed to go back to ancient Uralic mythology.

<h2> Viräva — the forest
The forest was of central importance in the life of the Moksha, as it was their main living space. The patroness of the forest was Viräva ("вирь" – forest + "ава" – woman) — a tall, black-haired, naked woman with long hair and breasts thrown over her shoulders. She could be kind and help a lost person find the way, or she could destroy them: tickle them to death or lead them astray in the thicket. Every forest had its own Viräva.
People prayed to her asking to be led out of the forest, to be shown where to gather berries or mushrooms, and to save those who were lost. Viräva holds a special place in folklore, turning into an artistic image close to that of a mythical woman from the depths of the forest.
Virätä ("атя" – old man), her husband, played a secondary role. Men going into the forest mentioned him as a protector against forest dangers or evil spirits.
<image> virjava.png | Viräva

<h2> Modava (Mastorava) — the earth. Paksäva — the field
For the farming Moksha people, the earth was a nurturing mother, connected not only with the harvest but also with health. Modava ("мода" – earth) is the female deity of the earth, endowed with a fertile power that played an important role in the well-being of the community. That is why sacrifices to her were made with special reverence, especially after the harvest or in times of illness. Old women healers used hops, millet and wine in these sacrifices. Her sending of illness is a belief that goes back to the fear of dampness and cold. In case of illness or a fall to the ground, one was supposed to ask her for forgiveness.
As society developed, the role of a field deity separated from that of the earth deity, and Paksäva ("пакся" – field) took it on. She dwelt on the field boundaries, and the places where she stayed were forbidden for sleeping and resting. She was revered as a provider and keeper of the harvest. In the middle of the harvest she was invited to dinner, and at the end she was thanked with food left for her. Sunstroke and crop failure were considered Paksäva's punishment.
Modava did not lose her importance, but prayers and sacrifices for the harvest and fertility came to be dedicated to Paksäva more often.
Mastorava ("мастор" – land, country) is considered the patroness of the land in a broader sense — of territory and peoples. Her name is also borne by the heroic epic created by folklorist A. Sharonov and published in 1994. The epic recreates the pre-Christian worldview of the Moksha and Erzya, combining scattered folk songs, myths, legends and laments into a single large-scale literary canvas.
<image> mastorava.jpg | Cover of "Mastorava"

<h2> Vedäva — water
Water was personified as Vedäva ("ведь" – water) — a pale-skinned woman with knee-length hair. Every river, lake or spring had its own Vedäva. In folklore she lives in deep waters and can drag a person under or send an illness upon them.
Sacrifices were made to her to ask for health, children and rain, and the prayers were held by the water. Morning water, "ведь пря", was of particular importance — it was used to wash the sick and to water livestock.
<image> vedjava.png | Vedäva

<h2> Tolava — fire
In Mokshan mythology, fire appeared as Tolava ("тол" – fire) — a dangerous but useful force. On the one hand, it warms, protects, and gives food and light; on the other, it can destroy whole villages, so Tolava was both revered and feared.
Tolava was considered the cause of fires, especially sudden ones. In times of disaster, offerings were thrown straight into the flames for her.
There was a tradition of putting out the household fire before a communal prayer and taking new fire from the sacrificial altar — as a sign of renewal and purification. Tolava was also associated with the home hearth, family warmth, and protection from darkness and evil forces. Hence the many protective rites involving fire: circling the bride, chests and bread with a burning splinter.
Tolava could also be a healing force. Heat and ashes from the sacred fire were considered protective and could be used in charms and purification rites.

<h2> Varmava — wind
In the traditional Moksha worldview, wind had a dangerous, destructive power. Varmava ("варма" – wind), the patroness of the wind, personified this element and could bring rain and relief from the heat, or cause a storm, a drought, or spread a fire when acting together with Tolava. She was perceived as a formidable yet necessary force of nature.
Varmava was appeased when winnowing grain and during droughts, with requests for a gentle wind. The same was done during fires: an offering to her was thrown into the flames. It was believed that if Varmava was favorable, the wind would change and the fire would spare the houses. Millers asked Varmava to blow wind onto the mill, and peasants asked her not to disturb the harvest. In particular, she was asked not to flatten the crops.
Illnesses were also associated with wind: wind was seen as a carrier of disease, and if a person fell ill after a strong draft, it was believed that Varmava had sent the illness. For healing, people turned to women healers, who recited special charms listing all the forces of nature, including Viräva, Vedäva and Modava.

<h2> Yurtava (Kudava) — the home
In Moksha beliefs, the home was a sacred space where ancestral and sacred lines intersected. Its patroness was Kudava ("куд" – house) — a female deity who guarded the house, the family, order and fire. Her image could be invisible but felt in everyday life — through rules of behavior, customs and taboos. Over time, the image of Yurtava ("юрхта" – house, yard) appeared — a small white woman who sees everything and knows everything. She lives under the stove or in the far corner of the house.
People prayed to Yurtava when laying the foundation of a new house, when moving, during wedding and funeral rites, when newlyweds first entered the house after the wedding, at the birth of a child, and when family members fell ill — about every important change in the lives of the household. She was given modest offerings. She was believed to guard the hearth, and her anger could cause illness or quarrels in the family.
Other deities lived alongside Yurtava. Kardazava ("кардаз" – yard), the keeper of the yard, protected the yard and the livestock and lived in the right-hand part of the yard. Boharämava ("бохарям" – cellar) was the deity of the cellar. Banäva ("баня" – bathhouse) was the patroness of the bathhouse.

<h2> The emergence of the idea of a supreme deity
Originally, Mokshan mythology had no supreme god — the pantheon was horizontal, without hierarchy. All the forces of nature acted in parallel and were patrons of their own elements. This reflected the structure of a society based on kinship equality.
Over time, under the influence of social changes and foreign religious systems, the Moksha developed the idea of a supreme deity — Shkay. Shkay (sometimes Värde Shkay, Otsyu Shkay, Otsyu Shkaybas, Shkabavas) often appears in later folk songs and rites as the name of the supreme god, the organizer of the world and the creator of the gods.
However, Shkay does not replace the traditional female figures. Rather, he serves as a unifying center in an increasingly complex religious worldview. His role is to gather and to guide, but not to command. He does not intervene in human life directly, as Viräva or Modava do, but acts through the other gods. His cult never became dominant — it complemented the polytheistic system, and he remained more of a mythological than a ritual figure. His name is used more often in epic songs than in prayers or prayer gatherings.
At the same time, the existing deities came to be perceived differently — no longer as patrons of their elements, but as owners or masters. That is why the term "азор" – master/mistress came to be added when addressing them: Viräva, for example, was addressed as Viräzorava ("вирь-азор-ава").

